Message-ID: <4071B9C0.10203@uvm.edu>

Burnham & Anderson, _Model selection & inference_, imply the following 
formula for the negative log likelihood of a linear model:

	(n*log(deviance(model)/n))/2

That gives quite a different answer, however, than

	-logLik(model)

What they explicitly wrote is:
        MLE of sigma^2 = sum(residuals(model)^2)/n
and
        AIC = n*log(MLE of sigma^2) + 2*K

Unfortunately they don't cite any sources. Can anyone clarify the 
relationship between the deviance and the log-likelihood of a linear 
model for me?

