Prof. Ripley,

I guess that R^2 from  the intercept model is always smaller than the 
R^2 from the non-intercept model. Below is what I thought. Please 
correct me if I misunderstood anything..

Let

 SSR = regression sum of squares from model with intercept

 SST = total sum of squares

 SSM = sum of squares due to the mean = (y1+...+yn)^2/n

Based on the definitions of S. R. Searle "Linear Models" (1997, Wiley, 
page 95):

  intercept model:  R^2 = (SSR-SSM)/(SST-SSM)  = A

  non-intercept model:  R^2 = SSR/SST = B

Since  SSR<SST, SSM is non-negative, A is always smaller (or no larger) 
than B.

