  ... why do Splus and Stata differ?
  
  The Wiebull model can either be expressed as a proportional hazards or
an accelerated failure time model.  These are just different parameterizations,
with no particular reason to choose one over another.

  The Splus survreg function uses the accelerated failure time version.  This
is a natural consequence of placing it into a family of acf models.  All the
parameters are as described in Kalbfleisch and Prentice, The statistical 
analysis of failure time models (a very well known book in the field).  In
this form one is modeling survival time, so a positive coefficient is a good
thing (live longer).
  Obviously, Stata is reporting out the PH parameterization.  In this form a
positive coefficient is a bad thing (higher death rate).

   There is a simple formula to convert from one to the other, in the case
of a Wiebull, which I don't remember off the top of my head at the moment,
(and I'm about to be late for a meeting).  

        Terry Therneau
         
  

