From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Fri Apr  9 11:42:07 2004
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Fri, 09 Apr 2004 12:42:07 +0100
Subject: Re: deviance and logLik for lm objects
In-Reply-To: <40744C87.1030508@uvm.edu>
References: <40744C87.1030508@uvm.edu>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.44.0404091233030.3561-100000@gannet.stats>

The short answer is the deviance is *not defined* for a Gaussian glm with
unknown dispersion parameter.  If Burnham & Anderson really have the
formula you say, it is not common usage.

Deviance is defined carefully in McCullagh & Nelder (1989) but it 
unfortunately much misused.  Informally it is 

constant - twice maximized log likelihood

but note that the both the constant and the dominating measure for the 
likelihood need to be defined for numerical values to be comparable.
For GLMs with known dispersion one, the `constant' is twice log likelihood 
for a saturated model.  Otherwise the informal definition is the *scaled* 
deviance for known dispersion.

On Wed, 7 Apr 2004, Kristian Omland wrote:

> Addressing my own question ...
> 
> > Burnham & Anderson, _Model selection & inference_, imply the following 
> > formula for the negative log likelihood of a linear model:
> >     (n*log(deviance(model)/n))/2
> > That gives quite a different answer, however, than
> >     -logLik(model)
> 
> Burnham & Anderson's formula simply disregards the constant
>       n/2*log(2*pi)+n/2
> which was provided in Volker Bahn's post. I am curious, though, where -- 
> in the S-Plus documentation, MASS, or elsewhere -- the relationship 
> between deviance and likelihood is described.

It is early in Chapter 7 of MASS, but it does not account for the 
misreadings of less careful authors (although it does try to account for 
some the the muddle in the original sources).

